    "https://saintleo.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/SLU/job/University-Campus/XMLNAME-2027-Spring-Orientation-Leader_R6587": {
      "title": "2027 Spring Orientation Leader",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nPart time\n\nJob Description Summary The position of Orientation Leader (OL) is one that allows a student to make a substantial contribution to Saint Leo University and a lasting first impression on incoming students. OLs help new students and their families in making a positive transition to Saint Leo University. Ols are extremely knowledgeable about campus resources and student success strategies. OLs must understand and support the goals and objectives of the New Student Orientation (NSO) program. Below are the basic OL job responsibilities: 1. University Representative 2. Team Member/Leader 3. Facilitator 4. Event Execution An OL is required to be on campus 1.5 to 2 weeks prior to the start of the upcoming semester for training and planning. ***In addition to uploading resume, the following documents are required for consideration. *** 1. Cover Letter 2. References (minimum of 2) Job Description REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Responsibilities: To serve as a positive role model and resource for new students and their families To initiate and develop an open communication with faculty, advisors, staff, students, and family members attending NSO To work with faculty and staff members, other OLs, and members of Saint Leo administration to facilitate the success of all aspects of the NSO program. To lead tours and discussion groups for incoming students and/or parents To serve as a representative of Saint Leo in a variety of situations including social functions and meals To assist in preparation, implementation and evaluation of NSO To perform other duties as deemed necessary by the Student Success professional staff To actively participate in training to become knowledgeable on various aspects of the University Mentor relationships with new students   QUALIFICATIONS: Minimum 2.5 cumulative GPA Current full-time student Good judicial standing A passion for helping others Ability to work independently as well as with a team, and a willingness to both lead and follow Interpersonal communication skills Reliability, creativity, stamina, flexibility/adaptability, and punctuality Ability to relate to and work with a variety of personality types and diverse groups of people (students, parents, faculty, both professional and student staff) A positive attitude and a sense of humor Ability to work under limited supervision Ability to work long hours (10-15hrs and/or early or late shifts) Able to handle constructive criticism Familiarity with Saint Leo programs and services   ENVIRONMENT:     The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation, can perform the essential functions of this position.   While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and University, handle various types of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 10 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description.   NOTICE: This job description intends to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description.  Saint Leo University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student, and graduate success.    Why Work at Saint Leo? What it’s Like to Work Here:  Ask our employees and the one word they’d use to describe working at Saint Leo University is “Community.” Our team members all share the Saint Leo core values, positive attitudes, and problem-solving abilities, enabling them to provide excellent student centered service. Our mission is educating and preparing students for life and leadership in a challenging world.  Thank you for your interest in joining the Saint Leo PRIDE! We are committed to providing our employees with the support they need. At Saint Leo, we offer an array of medical, dental, and vision packages as well as several add-on perks to make your benefits package truly customizable to you and your family needs. Available benefits based on employment status (Full Time vs Part Time). FREE Tuition - Employee, Spouse, and Dependents* Tuition Exchange Opportunity - Dependent of Employees* Generous Paid Leave - Sick, Vacation, and Holidays Comprehensive Group Health Plan (Medical, Dental, and Vision) Group Medical Plan includes Teledoc, Surgery Plus, Wellness Incentive Program and more! 100% Employer-Funded Health Reimbursement Account 100% Employer-Paid Short Term Disability Insurance 100% Employer-Funded Employee Assistance Program (healthcare and dependent options) Employer-provided life insurance Discounted On-Campus Dining Meal Plans Nationwide Pet Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts 403b Retirement Plan Wellness Center *Eligibility based on meeting required service period 2025 ICUBA Benefits Guide_St Leo"
    },
    "https://saintleo.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/SLU/job/University-Campus/Graduate-Nursing-Director_R6546": {
      "title": "Graduate Nursing Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJob Description Summary Saint Leo University seeks an experienced, mission-driven professional to serve as a twelve-month Graduate Nursing Director with significant graduate teaching responsibilities. This is a blended administrative and teaching faculty position, structured as approximately 50% program administration and 50% graduate-level teaching, reporting to the Dean, College of Health Professions. As Director, this individual provides strategic, academic, administrative, and operational leadership for the graduate nursing programs, ensuring program quality, regulatory compliance, student success, faculty development, curriculum oversight, accreditation readiness, and achievement of institutional goals. As teaching faculty, this individual carries a significant graduate teaching load, maintaining direct, sustained engagement with students across didactic, clinical, and laboratory/simulation instruction. The Director collaborates with faculty, university leadership, healthcare partners, and community stakeholders to advance excellence in nursing education and support the mission and values of the institution. The Graduate Director fosters an environment of academic excellence, innovation, scholarship, service, and continuous improvement while ensuring compliance with accreditation standards, state regulations, and professional nursing requirements. This is a twelve-month position based at the Saint Leo University campus in St. Leo, Florida. Appointment may be made at the assistant or associate professor level, Track TBD. Teaching load: consistent with the position's blended structure, the Director carries a reduced teaching assignment relative to a standard full-time faculty line — illustratively, roughly 7–8 credit hours per semester, approximately half of the standard 15-credit full-time teaching load described in Saint Leo's general graduate nursing faculty job description — combining didactic, clinical, and laboratory/simulation instruction across on-ground, online, and hybrid modalities, with the balance of the appointment devoted to administrative and program-leadership duties. Program context: this position is being created to lead two new graduate nursing programs — the Direct Entry MSN (DEMSN), launching January 2027, and the MSN–Family Nurse Practitioner (MSN-FNP), launching August 2027 — and is needed in place well before both launch dates. Beyond current master's-level operations, the Director also holds strategic responsibility for planning and preparing the College's future expansion into doctoral-level nursing education, including the eventual development, accreditation readiness, and operational oversight of a future doctoral nursing program, as the College's graduate portfolio continues to grow. The following duties, responsibilities, and required knowledge, skills, and abilities are specific to the Graduate Nursing Program Director role and supplement the general Department Chair/Director Responsibilities applicable to all chairs and directors at Saint Leo University. Job Description Administrative Leadership and Program Management Academic Leadership ● Monitor student learning outcomes and program effectiveness measures. ● Promote innovative teaching methodologies, simulation, technology-enhanced learning, and evidence-based educational practices. ● Ensure compliance with State Board of Nursing requirements and accreditation standards. ● Lead program reviews, self-studies, and accreditation activities. Faculty Leadership and Development ● Foster faculty engagement in teaching excellence, scholarship, professional development, and service. ● Support faculty orientation and onboarding activities. ● Promote a collaborative and inclusive academic culture. Student Success and Program Oversigh t ● Monitor student retention, progression, graduation, and licensure outcomes. ● Review and enforce nursing program policies and procedures. ● Support development of student leadership and professional engagement opportunities. Accreditation, Compliance, and Assessmen t ● Maintain compliance with nursing accreditation standards and state regulatory requirements. ● Coordinate data collection, analysis, and reporting for accreditation and assessment purposes. ● Monitor key performance indicators including NCLEX pass rates, retention rates, graduation rates, and employment outcomes. ● Ensure documentation and reporting requirements are completed accurately and timely. ● Develop and implement action plans to address identified areas for improvement. Clinical and Community Partnerships ● Establish and maintain effective relationships with healthcare organizations and clinical partners. ● Collaborate with healthcare agencies to secure and maintain clinical placement opportunities. ● Represent the nursing program in professional, educational, and community organizations. ● Promote partnerships that enhance experiential learning opportunities for students. Administrative and Budgetary Responsibilities ● Support graduate nursing education principles and best practices. ● Implement Nursing curriculum design, assessment, and program evaluation at the program level. ● Comply with Nursing accreditation standards and regulatory requirements. ● Comply with State Board of Nursing regulations and nursing licensure requirements. ● Address contemporary healthcare trends and workforce needs. ● Represent the nursing program professionally to internal and external stakeholders. ● Manage program budgets and personnel as assigned. Scholarship and Service ● Maintain an active record of scholarly and professional activities. ● Engage in professional nursing organizations and community service activities. ● Support faculty and student scholarship initiatives. Teaching Responsibilities Approximately 50% of this position is devoted to graduate-level teaching, structured as follows: ● Provide graduate-level classroom (didactic) instruction in assigned MSN, DEMSN, and/or MSN-FNP courses, delivered on-ground, online, and in hybrid modalities as appropriate. ● Provide clinical supervision and oversight of graduate nursing students in assigned clinical experiences, ensuring safe, competent practice consistent with program requirements and Board of Nursing standards. ● Provide laboratory and simulation instruction, including the use of nursing simulation software and equipment, to support hands-on clinical skill development. ● Advise and mentor graduate nursing students, supporting their academic progression, professional development, and engagement with the program. ● Participate in curriculum development and assessment for courses taught, including ongoing review and revision of course content, learning outcomes, and instructional methods. ● Participate in accreditation, program evaluation, and student success initiatives related to teaching, including contributing data, documentation, and evidence of teaching effectiveness to program-level accreditation and assessment activities. ● Maintain currency in nursing practice through research, certifications, and/or professional development relevant to courses taught. ● Teach traditional and non-traditional age students, and support program recruiting and retention efforts through direct student engagement. ● Serve on program and university committees as assigned, consistent with the position's faculty role. Doctoral Program Development and Strategic Grow th ● Provide strategic leadership and long-range planning for the College's future expansion into doctoral-level nursing education, building on current master's-level program operations. ● Lead planning, feasibility assessment, and preparation activities for future doctoral program development, in coordination with the Dean and university leadership. ● Direct the development, implementation, and accreditation-readiness activities for a future doctoral nursing program, including curriculum design and program evaluation infrastructure. ● Maintain operational oversight of doctoral program development and, upon implementation, ongoing doctoral program operations. ● Coordinate faculty recruitment and assignment, curriculum development, clinical placement arrangements, and regulatory and accreditation requirements associated with doctoral program growth. Qualifications Knowledge (Required) ● Earned doctorate in Nursing or a closely related field from a regionally accredited institution. ● Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) from an accredited institution. ● Current, unencumbered Registered Nurse (RN) license. ● Eligibility to obtain and maintain Florida Registered Nurse licensure. ● Minimum of five (5) years of professional nursing practice experience, with expertise in one or more of: long-term care, acute care, critical care, community settings, obstetrics, pediatrics, and/or mental health. ● Minimum of five (5) years of teaching experience in nursing education. ● Experience and credentials to teach nursing courses per accreditation guidelines. ● Demonstrated experience with curriculum development, program assessment, and accreditation processes. ● Demonstrated leadership experience in higher education or healthcare settings. Skills ● Demonstrated success with active learning strategies. ● Experience applying technology in teaching, including use of a learning management system. ● Utilization of discipline-related technology, including nursing simulation software and equipment, desired. ● Demonstrated effectiveness working collaboratively. Abilities ● Strong written and verbal communication skills. ● Effective interaction with a diverse student and colleague community. ● Willingness to take initiative and thrive in a dynamic environment. Additional Preferred Qualifications ● Experience serving in an academic leadership role such as program coordinator, assistant chair, chair, director, or dean. ● Certification as a family nurse practitioner. ● Experience with CCNE accreditation standards. ● Experience overseeing pre-licensure or graduate nursing programs. ● Record of scholarship, publications, presentations, and professional service. ● Experience with simulation-based nursing education. ● Experience managing budgets and personnel. Working Conditions ● Work is performed in office, classroom, simulation laboratory, clinical, and virtual learning environments. ● Occasional evening and weekend work may be required. ● Travel to clinical sites, professional meetings, and university events may be necessary. Application Instructions ● Submit a letter of interest, current CV, and contact information for three professional references. ● Review of applications begins immediately and continues until the position is filled, with a target hire date of Spring 2027. ● Official transcripts and verification of RN licensure will be required prior to a formal offer. Environment The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation, can perform the essential functions of this position. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and University, handle various type of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 10 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description. Notice The intent of this job description is to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Saint Leo University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student and graduate success. Why Work at Saint Leo? What it’s Like to Work Here:  Ask our employees and the one word they’d use to describe working at Saint Leo University is “Community.” Our team members all share the Saint Leo core values, positive attitudes, and problem-solving abilities, enabling them to provide excellent student centered service. Our mission is educating and preparing students for life and leadership in a challenging world.  Thank you for your interest in joining the Saint Leo PRIDE! We are committed to providing our employees with the support they need. At Saint Leo, we offer an array of medical, dental, and vision packages as well as several add-on perks to make your benefits package truly customizable to you and your family needs. Available benefits based on employment status (Full Time vs Part Time). FREE Tuition - Employee, Spouse, and Dependents* Tuition Exchange Opportunity - Dependent of Employees* Generous Paid Leave - Sick, Vacation, and Holidays Comprehensive Group Health Plan (Medical, Dental, and Vision) Group Medical Plan includes Teledoc, Surgery Plus, Wellness Incentive Program and more! 100% Employer-Funded Health Reimbursement Account 100% Employer-Paid Short Term Disability Insurance 100% Employer-Funded Employee Assistance Program (healthcare and dependent options) Employer-provided life insurance Discounted On-Campus Dining Meal Plans Nationwide Pet Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts 403b Retirement Plan Wellness Center *Eligibility based on meeting required service period 2025 ICUBA Benefits Guide_St Leo"
    },
    "https://saintleo.wd503.myworkdayjobs.com/SLU/job/University-Campus/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-Exercise-and-Sport-Sciences_R6501": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor-Exercise and Sport Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nJob Description Summary This position is founding faculty for Saint Leo University’s new Bachelor of Science in Exercise and Sport Science program, launching its first cohort in Fall 2027. This appointment is also part of the University’s Dynamic Faculty Model, a new category of 12-month faculty created to launch emerging programs and connect them directly with industry and community partners. For this Innovation Faculty role, Saint Leo seeks an innovator, an enthusiastic individual who is passionate about building and growing a program from the ground up. The Assistant or Associate Professor will teach undergraduate Exercise and Sport Science courses while helping shape the program’s curriculum, course sequencing, assessment design, and continuous improvement from inception, including the opportunity to lead the program’s accreditation strategy through the Commission on Accreditation of Allied Health Education Programs (CAAHEP) under standards set by the Committee on Accreditation for the Exercise Sciences (CoAES). The role also includes student advising, experiential learning supervision, recruitment, and partnership-building with healthcare, fitness, sport, and performance organizations across the Tampa Bay region. This is a full-time, twelve-month, renewable faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank (rank and salary commensurate with the incumbent's experience), non-tenure track, based at Saint Leo University’s main campus in St. Leo, Florida. Salary is established on a twelve-month basis and indexed to reflect the full annual term of the appointment. The position has no direct reports and reports to the Department Chairperson and/or Dean. Job Description Duties and Tasks Teaching and Student Learning Teach undergraduate courses in Exercise and Sport Science and related areas. Deliver instruction through traditional, hybrid, online, laboratory, and experiential learning formats. Develop and implement evidence-based instructional strategies that support student success and engagement. Utilize educational technologies and discipline-specific software to enhance teaching and learning outcomes. Assess student achievement and continuously improve course quality through data-informed practices. Program Development and Assessment Participate in shaping the program from its inception, including curriculum design, course sequencing, and accreditation strategy. Support curriculum development, revision, and assessment initiatives. Participate in accreditation, compliance, and continuous quality improvement processes. Contribute to strategic initiatives that support enrollment growth, retention, and student achievement. Lead program-building efforts that establish the program’s identity, visibility, and market presence from its first cohort forward. Work directly with internal and external stakeholders, including admissions, marketing, and industry and community partners, to achieve program enrollment targets. Assist with development of new certificates, concentrations, and industry partnerships. Student Advising and Mentorship Advise and mentor undergraduate students regarding academic progression, career development, and professional preparation. Support students pursuing graduate education, professional certifications, and careers in health, fitness, sport performance, rehabilitation, and allied health professions. Supervise internships, field experiences, capstone projects, and undergraduate research activities. Scholarship and Professional Engagement Maintain professional currency through scholarship, certification, professional practice, and continuing education. Engage in scholarly activities, presentations, or applied professional projects. Participate in professional organizations and maintain visibility within the discipline. Service and Community Engagement Serve on departmental, college, and university committees. Develop and maintain relationships with healthcare systems, fitness organizations, sport organizations, rehabilitation centers, community agencies, and professional partners across the Tampa Bay region. Support student recruitment, retention, alumni engagement, and community outreach initiatives. Perform other duties as assigned by the Department Chairperson and/or Dean. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Knowledge Master’s degree in Exercise Science, Kinesiology, or a closely related movement science field, with significant professional experience and eligibility for faculty appointment under institutional and accreditation guidelines. (Required) Specialization in one or more of the following is preferred: Exercise Physiology, Strength and Conditioning, Human Performance, Sport Performance, Biomechanics, Clinical Exercise Physiology, Sport and Exercise Psychology, Sport Nutrition, or Motor Learning and Motor Control. Minimum of three years of professional experience in higher education, clinical exercise physiology, strength and conditioning, sport performance, health promotion, rehabilitation, fitness management, or related settings. (Required) Professional certification is preferred (e.g., ACSM-EP or ACSM-CEP, NSCA-CSCS, CPSS, NASM or ACE credentials, or other nationally recognized certification relevant to the discipline); multiple certifications are highly desirable, though certification is not required to teach. Knowledge of exercise physiology and human performance assessment, exercise prescription for healthy and clinical populations, strength and conditioning principles, applied biomechanics and movement analysis, evidence-based health promotion and wellness strategies, and current industry and accreditation standards within exercise and sport science. Preferred: knowledge of sports analytics and performance-monitoring technologies, wearable technology and physiological data collection, clinical exercise testing and interpretation, program assessment and accreditation processes, and emerging trends in sport science and human performance optimization. Skills Demonstrated excellence in teaching, training, coaching, mentoring, or professional supervision, with strong oral, written, and interpersonal communication. Experience utilizing contemporary educational technologies and discipline-specific software in teaching and assessment. Ability to integrate technology and active-learning strategies into instruction, with data-driven decision-making and assessment skills. Strong organizational and project management abilities, and effective collaboration across interdisciplinary teams. Preferred: experience teaching in higher education, supervising internships or experiential learning placements, and disseminating scholarly work. Preferred: experience with curriculum development, accreditation, program assessment, and outcomes measurement, and experience using human performance testing equipment and laboratory technologies. Abilities Ability to cultivate partnerships with industry, healthcare, fitness, sport, and community stakeholders, including organizations across the Tampa Bay region. Ability to foster an inclusive, supportive learning environment and mentor students from diverse educational, cultural, and professional backgrounds, including those pursuing graduate education, professional certifications, and careers in health, fitness, sport performance, rehabilitation, and allied health. Ability to lead curriculum innovations and program growth initiatives, including shaping a new program from inception and leading its accreditation strategy. Ability to adapt to evolving educational technologies and workforce needs, and to demonstrate initiative, professionalism, and commitment to continuous improvement. Ability to work effectively with diverse populations across healthcare, fitness, performance, and community settings. REQUIRED DOCUMENTS In addition to your curriculum vitae, the following documents are required for consideration. Please be sure to check the email under which you applied for important instructions on completing remaining steps, including uploading documents in the second step of the application packet process as follows: Cover Letter Teaching Philosophy - (500 words or less), should highlight how your teaching philosophy and practices fit the Mission and Values of Saint Leo University. Three (3) Letters of Recommendation – All letters of recommendation should be professional references and at least one (1) from a recent superior. If you do not have those documents at the time of application, please upload a text or word document stating, \"Recommendations will be sent independently.\" Recommendation letters can be mailed to Academic Affairs at the address stated below Student Evaluations (six classes minimum) – Student evaluations from the last year (at least six classes, an even mix of graduate and undergraduate). TRANSCRIPTS Official transcripts are required for all adjuncts and all final full-time faculty candidates Official transcripts for Master, Graduate, and Doctorate degrees are required to complete your application packet and are to be issued directly to Saint Leo University from the respective education institution.* Transcripts that are “Issued to Student” or issued to another institution are not accepted. Digital and/or electronic transcripts cannot be accepted at this time. World Education Services (WES) Evaluation – A certified evaluation and translation from World Education Services (WES) is required for foreign transcripts and/or any and all degrees obtained from international universities. Expenses for official transcripts are the responsibility of the applicant. Please have transcripts and certified evaluations mailed to: Attn: Veronica Mantegna Academic Affairs – MC 2006 Saint Leo University PO Box 6665 Saint Leo, Florida 33574 Full-time faculty positions are pending final budget approval prior to start of the academic year. Environment The work environment characteristics described here are a representation of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. The term “qualified individual with a disability” means an individual with a disability who, with or without reasonable accommodation, can perform the essential functions of this position. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to communicate professionally in person, over the telephone, through email and other electronic means, move about the office and University, handle various type of media and equipment, and visually or otherwise identify, observe and assess. The employee is occasionally required to lift up to 10 pounds unless otherwise specified in the job description. Notice The intent of this job description is to provide a representation of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Saint Leo University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and embraces diversity as a critical step in ensuring employee, student and graduate success. Why Work at Saint Leo? What it’s Like to Work Here:  Ask our employees and the one word they’d use to describe working at Saint Leo University is “Community.” Our team members all share the Saint Leo core values, positive attitudes, and problem-solving abilities, enabling them to provide excellent student centered service. Our mission is educating and preparing students for life and leadership in a challenging world.  Thank you for your interest in joining the Saint Leo PRIDE! We are committed to providing our employees with the support they need. At Saint Leo, we offer an array of medical, dental, and vision packages as well as several add-on perks to make your benefits package truly customizable to you and your family needs. Available benefits based on employment status (Full Time vs Part Time). FREE Tuition - Employee, Spouse, and Dependents* Tuition Exchange Opportunity - Dependent of Employees* Generous Paid Leave - Sick, Vacation, and Holidays Comprehensive Group Health Plan (Medical, Dental, and Vision) Group Medical Plan includes Teledoc, Surgery Plus, Wellness Incentive Program and more! 100% Employer-Funded Health Reimbursement Account 100% Employer-Paid Short Term Disability Insurance 100% Employer-Funded Employee Assistance Program (healthcare and dependent options) Employer-provided life insurance Discounted On-Campus Dining Meal Plans Nationwide Pet Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts 403b Retirement Plan Wellness Center *Eligibility based on meeting required service period 2025 ICUBA Benefits Guide_St Leo"
